## 9. BEHAVIOURAL ECONOMICS AND ENGAGEMENT ENGINE

Design MEBT to encourage consistent, voluntary use through genuine customer value.

The objective is to make productive administrative behaviour easier, more rewarding and more likely to occur.

Apply the following principles systematically throughout the experience.

### 9.1 Cognitive ease

Reduce the mental effort required to understand and complete administrative tasks.

- Use simple language.
- Break complex processes into manageable steps.
- Present information progressively.
- Replace long instructions with contextual guidance.
- Use illustrations and examples where useful.
- Avoid overwhelming users with unnecessary decisions.

### 9.2 Choice architecture

Structure available options so users can easily identify the most relevant action.

- Prioritise urgent and actionable tasks.
- Recommend an appropriate next step using deterministic rules.
- Explain why the action is recommended.
- Allow users to override, postpone or dismiss recommendations.
- Avoid excessive choices on a single screen.

### 9.3 Goal-gradient effect

Make progress towards meaningful objectives visible.

- Display completed checklist items.
- Show remaining actions.
- Use milestone indicators.
- Celebrate meaningful administrative accomplishments.
- Show progress across individual cases.

Progress indicators must represent user-completed tasks only, never the probability of legal approval or official government processing progress.

### 9.4 Implementation intentions

Help users convert intentions into specific actions.

Allow users to create plans such as:

- Follow up on my application on Monday.
- Prepare my supporting documents tomorrow.
- Review my outstanding requirements this weekend.

Provide date selection, scheduling and simulated reminders.

### 9.5 Reduction of friction

Minimise unnecessary effort.

- Preserve progress automatically.
- Resume incomplete workflows.
- Reuse fictional demonstration information.
- Provide relevant templates.
- Offer contextual quick actions.
- Reduce unnecessary typing.
- Make navigation reversible.
- Provide undo where appropriate.

### 9.6 Positive reinforcement

Provide meaningful feedback when users complete productive actions.

Examples:

- Checklist item completed.
- Follow-up recorded.
- Document organised.
- Administrative blocker addressed.
- Case preparation milestone reached.

Use restrained visual celebrations and encouraging messages.

Do not reward meaningless app opens, excessive screen time or repetitive interactions.

### 9.7 Salience and timely prompts

Surface important information at the appropriate moment.

Examples:

- Upcoming document expiry.
- Outstanding follow-up.
- Missing supporting information.
- Unresolved administrative blocker.
- Scheduled action.

Provide clear urgency distinctions without unnecessarily alarming users.

Allow users to control reminder frequency, snooze reminders and disable nonessential notifications.

### 9.8 Self-efficacy

Help users feel increasingly capable of managing their own administrative affairs.

Explain why actions matter.

Provide contextual educational guidance.

Highlight genuine accomplishments.

Use reassuring, empowering language without making promises about legal outcomes.

### 9.9 Personal relevance

Adapt the interface according to the user's optional demonstration status, selected objectives, active cases and language preferences.

Do not force disclosure of immigration status.

Do not infer sensitive information.

### 9.10 Trust and uncertainty reduction

Clearly distinguish between:

- User-recorded information.
- Demonstration information.
- Pending verification.
- Completed user actions.
- Official information, if independently verified in a future release.

Never fabricate official statuses, legal requirements or institutional responses.

### 9.11 Ethical engagement

Do not implement:

- Artificial urgency.
- Fear-based monetisation.
- Manipulative streak penalties.
- Gambling-style reward mechanisms.
- Fake scarcity.
- Shame-based notifications.
- Compulsive engagement loops.
- Misleading social proof.
- Obstructive cancellation or exit mechanisms.

Optimise for successful administrative outcomes, task completion, comprehension, confidence and voluntary return visits rather than time spent in the application.

